          "assessmentGuideline": "Acting Out, the person is completely at the mercy of his emotions. The effect upon him or her of the stimulus is so great that (s)he becomes a puppet who acts out some experience which overwhelmed him or her. If you really want to see people acting out, go down to the local nut house, but a child throwing a tantrum is just as good an example. An even more graphic example is an adult throwing a tantrum. This may be a more common occurrence than you think. People who do this usually get into a lot of trouble. They tend to wind up in jails, hospitals and/or courts of law. When a person is acting out all the time, he is definitely a psychiatric case. An inmate who believes that he is Teddy Roosevelt, or that the Earth has been invaded by Martians, is acting out. Even for experts, it is usually very difficult to figure out what such a person is acting out. But (s)he is acting out some incidents) in which (s)he is mentally immersed. That incident may be more real to him or her than present time and space. When a person has a flashback or hallucination, (s)he is out of present time and space for the duration of the episode. One can cavalierly, but accurately, call it delusion. But it is more useful to call it \"out of present time and space,\" because the person is reacting to something very real for him or her, even if that something is not observable to the rest of us. The incident which the person is acting out has a hypnotic hold over him or her. One plays it back like a phonograph record or video. There is no volition American comedy team of Abbott and Costello. It goes like this: The comedians are in an enclosed area with some stranger. When Costello mentions Niagara Falls, the stranger gets a crazed look and begins, \"Slowly I turned, step by step...\", etc., and acts out how he murdered someone. Costello is in danger of physical harm, but he is rescued from the stranger by Abbott. After the stranger is quieted down, Abbott asks Costello: \"What did you do?\" Costello says: \"It don't know. All I said was Niagara Falls.\" Immediately, the stranger begins again: \"Slowly I turned, step by step...\" etc., and the exact same behavior ensues. This classic routine of immense hilarity is a very good, if light-hearted, demonstration of Level VII, Acting Out, behavior. You can catch it on reruns of Abbott and Costello's 1950's television show. There is no point in talking to someone who is acting out, as it will do no good, and could be dangerous. Just up the scale from acting out is Level VI, Shock. You tell mama that her child was just killed in an accident, and she goes into shock. She may even pass out as a result. Or you come home from work early and unannounced to find, shockingly, your spouse in bed with some stranger. Formerly, according to Italian law, a wronged spouse who found his or her spouse in flagrante dilecto and murdered one or both of the lovers on the spot was exonerated, as it was considered justifiable homicide. This was known informally as \"divorce Italian- style.\" Such a reaction in an Italian was considered understandable. Legalization of divorce in Italy did away with such a defense. Clearly, shock is a more appropriate reaction than acting out. But the reaction is severe enough to completely short-circuit the Perhaps catatonics are individuals who are somehow chronically in severe shock. That certainly is how they appear. A perso Level VI cannot compute. He is just shut down from sensory overload. The intensity of the stimulus is more than his or her circu could handle, so (s)he blows a fuse, so to speak. Whether or not the lights are on, nobody is home. Do not try to talk to such an individual, other than to get him or her to a safe place where (s)he can recover. (S)he is, to so degree, in a hypnotic state. Anything you say around him could have the effect of a post-hypnotic suggestion, and will only m him or her worse. After you have discovered a few of these inadvertent post-hypnotic commands on people, you will have certa about that. The victim cannot process the data that caused him or her to go into shock. You know that, for sure, because (s)he i shock. If (s)he could have processed the data, (s)he would not be in shock. Just above that is Upset, Level V. This person might shout at you, but might just fume speechlessly instead. This is a comr reaction for most people. It is too much to expect someone to never get upset. But it is reasonable to expect an upset perso control oneself. A reasonably well-adjusted person can control oneself when (s)he gets upset. Don't try to help this person talking to him. Just listen and acknowledge. Facilitators, psychotherapists, ministers and bartenders should understand this most of their clientele are upset about something. If you just leave this person alone for a while, and allow the person to eat sleep, and perhaps say whatever (s)he wishes, (s)he will come out of the upset eventually. At Level IV is Rationalization. This person is trying to convince you, and oneself, that his or her actions are justified, and hi her position is reasonable. At this level, one gets a good look at how inventive, creative and clever the human mind can Anything can be rationalized. If you listen carefully, you will hear people gratuitously proffer their rationalizations. Do not buy what this snake oil salesma selling. Just be polite, listen and acknowledge. If you do, very often the rationalizer will realize how foolish (s)he sounds, or sim get it off his or her chest, and forget it. People in the helping professions are deluged by rationalizations. Very often, excuses are related to rationalizations. That is, excuser is trying to convince oneself that (s)he is right while (s)he is telling the excuse. A boldfaced liar is actually in somen better condition, if (s)he is not so foolish to believe one's own \"PR\" (Public Relations). A person who does believe one's c rationalizations is a pathological liar.",
